<emu-clause id="sec-authentication" normative-optional>
  <h1>Authentication</h1>

  <p>
    This Standard does not require a TEA server to authenticate its users,
    however TEA servers wishing to implement authentication shall conform to
    the entire Clause.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A TEA server that requires authentication shall implement the
      */token* endpoint (<emu-xref href="#sec-tea-op-requesttoken">`POST /token`</emu-xref>).</li>
    <li>A TEA server that requires authentication shall support API key
      credential exchange.</li>
    <li>A TEA server may support additional credential types on the same
      endpoint, such as federated identity from an external OpenID Connect or
      SAML provider, mutual TLS, or others.</li>
    <li>A TEA server shall ensure that a client which holds an API key is able
      to reach every endpoint for which it is authorized using exclusively the
      baseline exchange.</li>
    <li>A TEA server shall accept an API key exclusively on the
      */token* endpoint.</li>
    <li>A TEA server shall exchange an API key provided to the
      */token* endpoint for an access token to be provided to all
      other TEA API endpoints.</li>
    <li>Regardless of the credential type with which a client initiates its
      connection, the TEA server shall provide it with an HTTP bearer token
      allowing for interoperability across TEA clients and servers.</li>
  </ul>

  <emu-note>
    <p>
      There is no IETF specification for API keys, and no registered HTTP
      authentication scheme for them. The widespread _X-API-Key_
      header is additionally at odds with RFC 6648, a Best Current Practice
      that deprecates the `X-` prefix for new header fields. This
      specification therefore carries the API key in the standard
      _Authorization_ header using the Basic scheme, which is
      registered, specified, and supported directly by virtually every HTTP
      client library.
    </p>
  </emu-note>

  <emu-clause id="sec-token-endpoint">
    <h1>*/token* endpoint</h1>

    <p>
      A TEA server shall provide an authentication endpoint at
      <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-op-requesttoken">`POST /token`</emu-xref>,
      relative to the TEA API base URL. This endpoint shall be implemented as
      an OAuth 2.0 token endpoint as defined in RFC 6749 section 3.2. and
      provide an authorized client with a bearer token in response.
    </p>

    <p>
      Credentials and bearer tokens are transmitted in the clear at the HTTP
      layer, so a TEA server shall be reachable only over TLS.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-access-tokens">
    <h1>Access tokens</h1>

    <p>
      A TEA server shall accept from authenticated clients the HTTP bearer token
      in the appropriate HTTP header on all other TEA endpoints.
    </p>

    <emu-example>
      <pre><code class="http">GET /product/d4d9f54a-abcf-11ee-ac79-1a52914d44b1 HTTP/1.1
Host: tea.example.com
Authorization: Bearer 2YotnFZFEjr1zCsicMWpAA
</code></pre>
    </emu-example>

    <ul>
      <li>A TEA server should return _expires\_in_, so a client is able
        to avoid discovering expiry through a failed request.</li>
      <li>If a request to a resource endpoint fails with *401* and the
        _WWW-Authenticate_ challenge header carries
        *error="invalid_token"*, the client may obtain a new
        token from the token endpoint and retry the request once, as described in
        RFC 6750 section 3.1.</li>
    </ul>

    <emu-example>
      <pre><code class="http">HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized
  WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=&quot;tea&quot;, error=&quot;invalid_token&quot;, error_description=&quot;The access token expired&quot;
</code></pre>
    </emu-example>

    <p>
      This Standard defines no revocation endpoint, and clients need not
      implement one.
    </p>

    <p>
      Servers should not issue refresh tokens with the
      _client\_credentials_ grant, following RFC 6749 section 4.4.3. A
      TEA client holds its own API key and may simply call the token endpoint
      again.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-optional-credential">
    <h1>Optional credential types</h1>

    <p>
      A server may accept credentials other than an API key at the
      */token* endpoint, selected by the _grant\_type_
      parameter. The server shall provide the same token response regardless
      of credential type.
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that a server which delegates identity to an external provider
      shall still issue TEA access tokens from its token endpoint. The external
      provider authenticates the user; all TEA server endpoints maintain
      consistent authenticated behaviour.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-client-flow">
    <h1>Client flow</h1>

    <p>
      In TEA, resource operations may be called with a Bearer token or without
      one; a *401* with a *WWW-Authenticate: Bearer*
      challenge on a given endpoint is the runtime signal that a token is
      required.
    </p>

    <p>The complete flow for a naive TEA client is as follows:</p>

    <emu-alg>
      1. [id="step-no-credential"] The client sends a resource request with no credentials.
      1. If the server does not require authentication for that endpoint, it responds with the resource and the HTTP status code *200*. (The client is done).
      1. [id="step-401-response"] If the server requires authentication, it responds with HTTP status code *401* and a _WWW-Authenticate_ header naming the _Bearer_ scheme, as described in RFC 6750 section 3.
      1. The client requests <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-op-requesttoken">`POST /token`</emu-xref> with its valid credential and receives an access token.
      1. The client repeats the initial resource request along with HTTP header `Authorization: Bearer &lt;access_token>`. The client provides that same token on subsequent requests until it expires or is rejected.
      1. If a later request fails with HTTP status code *401* and *error="invalid_token"* within the HTTP header, the client obtains a fresh token and retries the request.
    </emu-alg>

    <p>
      A client that already holds an API key for a service may skip steps
      <emu-xref href="#step-no-credential"></emu-xref> to
      <emu-xref href="#step-401-response"></emu-xref> and call the token
      endpoint first: being issued a key is itself the signal that the service requires authentication.
    </p>

    <p>
      A client shall not probe */token* to find out whether
      authentication exists; the challenge on the resource is the appropriate
      discovery mechanism.
    </p>

    <p>
      A client similarly shall not send its API key directly to a resource
      endpoint; resource endpoints shall only accept bearer tokens.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-partially-protected">
    <h1>Partially secured resources</h1>

    <p>
      A TEA server may make some data available without authenticating while
      requiring authentication and authorization for other objects. Such a
      server requires authentication for the protected objects:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>the server shall implement the token endpoint and the baseline exchange;</li>
      <li>endpoints which do not require authentication ignore request tokens;</li>
      <li>protected endpoints shall respond with HTTP status code
        *401* and a *Bearer* challenge header if no valid
        token is presented;</li>
      <li>servers shall not respond with a concealing HTTP status code
        *404* solely because the client is unauthenticated.</li>
    </ul>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-protected-resource">
    <h1>Protected resource metadata</h1>

    <p>
      Servers which delegate identity to an external provider may publish
      OAuth 2.0 protected resource metadata (RFC 9728) and reference it from
      the *WWW-Authenticate* challenge, allowing a client to locate
      the authorization server automatically. This is optional; it does not
      replace the token endpoint, which remains the interoperable baseline,
      and it does not replace the challenge as a manner of discovering
      authentication is required.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>
